WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.425
Each layer of a transformer
has two blocks.

2
00:00:03.425 --> 00:00:07.550
The first is attention,
from the companion video.

3
00:00:07.550 --> 00:00:12.575
The second is a small neural
network, applied at every position.

4
00:00:12.775 --> 00:00:15.100
Let's open one up.

5
00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:20.900
It turns out to be two matrix products,
with one nonlinear step in between.

6
00:00:22.467 --> 00:00:25.778
Here is a tiny network,
from Activity 3.5.23:

7
00:00:25.778 --> 00:00:29.742
two inputs, three
neurons, and two outputs.

8
00:00:29.942 --> 00:00:33.367
Feed in the input x equals one, two.

9
00:00:33.567 --> 00:00:39.242
Each neuron starts with a dot product:
its weights times the inputs, added up.

10
00:00:39.242 --> 00:00:42.120
The first neuron has weights one and zero:

11
00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:45.867
one times one, plus
zero times two, is one.

12
00:00:46.067 --> 00:00:49.942
The second has weights zero
and one, and gets two.

13
00:00:50.142 --> 00:00:55.992
The third has weights one and one: one
times one, plus one times two, is three.

14
00:00:56.692 --> 00:01:01.017
Stack the weights as the
rows of a matrix, W one.

15
00:01:01.217 --> 00:01:05.779
Then all three dot products at
once are a single matrix product:

16
00:01:05.779 --> 00:01:09.217
W one times x equals one, two, three.

17
00:01:09.917 --> 00:01:12.467
Call this vector s.

18
00:01:13.667 --> 00:01:19.267
Next, each neuron applies the sigmoid
function, sigma, to its own number.

19
00:01:19.267 --> 00:01:24.217
Sigma squashes every number into
the interval from zero to one.

20
00:01:24.417 --> 00:01:27.617
One becomes about zero point seven three,

21
00:01:27.617 --> 00:01:30.592
two becomes about zero point eight eight,

22
00:01:30.592 --> 00:01:34.217
and three becomes about
zero point nine five.

23
00:01:34.617 --> 00:01:38.764
Sigma acts on each coordinate
separately, and it is not linear.

24
00:01:38.764 --> 00:01:41.342
This is the nonlinear step.

25
00:01:42.042 --> 00:01:43.292
Call the result u.

26
00:01:43.292 --> 00:01:47.842
The outputs are another matrix
product, W two times u.

27
00:01:48.042 --> 00:01:50.343
Each output is again a dot product.

28
00:01:50.343 --> 00:01:52.710
The first is zero point seven three,

29
00:01:52.710 --> 00:01:58.442
minus one half of zero point nine five,
which is about zero point two five.

30
00:01:59.142 --> 00:02:01.912
So the block is: multiply by W one,

31
00:02:01.912 --> 00:02:07.067
apply sigma to each coordinate,
then multiply by W two.

32
00:02:08.967 --> 00:02:13.067
Now watch the whole block act
on a flat grid of inputs.

33
00:02:13.267 --> 00:02:17.667
W one sends the plane into
three dimensional space.

34
00:02:17.667 --> 00:02:21.717
The grid stays flat, and its lines
stay straight and evenly spaced.

35
00:02:21.717 --> 00:02:24.392
That is what linear looks like.

36
00:02:25.592 --> 00:02:28.692
Now apply sigma to each coordinate.

37
00:02:28.692 --> 00:02:31.406
The sheet bends to fit
inside the unit cube,

38
00:02:31.406 --> 00:02:35.167
and its lines curve and
bunch up near the faces.

39
00:02:37.367 --> 00:02:42.217
Finally, W two maps the bent
sheet back down to the plane.

40
00:02:43.917 --> 00:02:48.667
The output grid is curved: the
block as a whole is not linear.

41
00:02:50.567 --> 00:02:53.467
But zoom in near the input zero.

42
00:02:53.467 --> 00:02:57.617
The closer you look, the
straighter the grid lines become.

43
00:02:59.817 --> 00:03:05.092
Up close, the block looks linear, and
its local matrix is the Jacobian.

44
00:03:05.492 --> 00:03:10.220
The chain rule says the Jacobian
is a product of three matrices,

45
00:03:10.220 --> 00:03:14.492
one for each step: W two,
times D, times W one.

46
00:03:15.192 --> 00:03:19.892
W one and W two are the
same matrices as before.

47
00:03:19.892 --> 00:03:22.224
The middle factor, D, is diagonal.

48
00:03:22.224 --> 00:03:26.867
Each entry is the slope of
sigma at that neuron's input.

49
00:03:27.267 --> 00:03:30.794
At zero, sigma is steepest,
with slope one quarter.

50
00:03:30.794 --> 00:03:34.292
So D is one quarter times the identity.

51
00:03:34.692 --> 00:03:40.292
Multiply it out: the Jacobian is
one quarter of W two times W one:

52
00:03:40.292 --> 00:03:44.542
one eighth, negative one eighth;
one eighth, three eighths.

53
00:03:45.242 --> 00:03:46.407
Notice the order.

54
00:03:46.407 --> 00:03:50.726
Data passes through W one first,
and W one stands on the right,

55
00:03:50.726 --> 00:03:54.817
because the factor next
to the input acts first.

56
00:03:56.700 --> 00:03:59.825
Now move the base point to two, zero.

57
00:03:59.825 --> 00:04:03.900
The inputs to sigma become
two, zero, and two.

58
00:04:03.900 --> 00:04:05.607
Out there, sigma is flatter,

59
00:04:05.607 --> 00:04:10.125
and its slope drops from one
quarter to about zero point one.

60
00:04:10.525 --> 00:04:16.200
Two entries of D shrink, the Jacobian
changes, and the local grid is squeezed.

61
00:04:16.900 --> 00:04:22.100
Same block, different matrix: the
local matrix depends on where you are.

62
00:04:23.967 --> 00:04:26.992
Here is the whole block in one line.

63
00:04:26.992 --> 00:04:31.058
Multiply by W one: each
neuron takes a dot product.

64
00:04:31.058 --> 00:04:32.417
Linear.

65
00:04:32.617 --> 00:04:34.768
Apply sigma to each coordinate.

66
00:04:34.768 --> 00:04:37.442
This is the nonlinear step.

67
00:04:37.642 --> 00:04:40.872
Multiply by W two: dot products again.

68
00:04:40.872 --> 00:04:42.267
Linear.

69
00:04:42.467 --> 00:04:46.310
Up close, the block is a
single matrix, W two D W one,

70
00:04:46.310 --> 00:04:50.242
and that matrix changes
from point to point.

71
00:04:50.642 --> 00:04:56.242
In a transformer, this block runs at
every position, right after attention.
